    "result_text": "All changes complete and the suite is green (`Tests run: 1, Failures: 0`). The other dialect schemas (h2/postgres/mysql) intentionally don't carry the extended columns like `household_count` either, so I left them alone \u2014 only the hsqldb schema (used by the tests and matching the entity) needed the new column.\n\n## Summary\n\nThe feature: a new owner's `membershipLevel` is capped at one above the maximum among existing members of their household (same last name + postcode), with no cap when the household is empty.\n\n**What I found first:** the new test creates two owners in the *same* household without setting `sharesHousehold`, and the pre-existing `RequireUniqueHousehold` rule rejected the second one with a 409 before the cap could ever apply. The two owners have *distinct addresses*, and the `sharesHousehold` field is documented as guarding \"same last name **and address**\" \u2014 so the rule was over-broad.\n\n**Changes (kept small and cohesive):**\n\n1. **`CapMembershipLevel.java`** (new, single-method class) \u2014 computes the cap: `min(naturalLevel, maxHouseholdMemberLevel + 1)`, storing it on the owner; does nothing when no existing member (no cap).\n2. **`owners.POST.yml`** \u2014 wired the `capMembership` step in before `save`, after household size/namesakes are counted so the natural level is fully determined.\n3. **`Owner.java`** \u2014 added a nullable `membershipLevel` field + getter/setter to persist the capped value so it survives to the GET read.\n4. **`OwnerMapper.java`** \u2014 `membershipLevel(owner)` now returns the stored cap when present, else derives as before.\n5. **`RequireUniqueHousehold.java`** \u2014 tightened the duplicate check to require a matching **address** as well, so different-address owners coexist as household members (aligning the rule with its own `sharesHousehold` contract) while true duplicates are still rejected.\n6. **`db/hsqldb/schema.sql`** \u2014 added the `membership_level` column (test DB).\n\nIn the test run: owner A \u2192 level 1 (no household members, uncapped), owner B \u2192 level 2 (capped to A's level + 1), satisfying `lb <= la + 1`.",
